UPDATE companies SET description='The initiative launched on August 1, 2018, when the utility transformed a former landfill off I-65 into Nashville''s first community solar park. The project began after customers expressed demand for renewable energy options, prompting NES to develop a shared solar facility that would allow broad participation without rooftop installations.

The program manages a 17,020-panel solar array that feeds clean energy directly into the NES grid. Customers purchase subscriptions to individual panels and receive a monthly solar credit on their electric bills through 2038. As of the latest data, 3,142 panels are leased to customers, generating over 21 million kilowatt-hours to date. The park also reserves panels for the Solar Angel Fund, a donation-based component that provides energy assistance to low-income households; 1,015 panels have been donated so far, with 12,863 panels still available for new subscribers.

The service targets residential customers — both homeowners and renters — across the NES service territory who want renewable energy without upfront installation costs or maintenance. It also serves low-income households through the Solar Angel donation channel, administered in partnership with The Community Foundation of Middle Tennessee.

NES frames the program as a direct response to customer voice: "The city spoke. We listened." The utility emphasizes maintenance-free participation, long-term bill credits, and a community-focused model that combines environmental impact with local energy assistance. The park''s location on a reclaimed landfill underscores a dual benefit of land reuse and emissions reduction.', certifications=ARRAY[]::text[], profile='{"capabilities": ["Community solar park operation", "Solar panel subscription management", "Monthly energy credit billing", "Solar Angel donation program administration", "Renewable energy grid integration"], "industries": ["Clean energy", "Residential energy services", "Low-income energy assistance"], "materials": [], "equipment": [], "volume": "", "lead_time": "", "tolerances": "", "built_on": ["First community solar park in Nashville", "17,020 panels on a reclaimed landfill site", "Monthly bill credits guaranteed through 2038", "Solar Angel donation program for low-income energy assistance", "No rooftop installation or maintenance required for subscribers"], "facts_used": ["Headquarters: Madison, Tennessee -> db", "Program launch: August 1, 2018 -> /", "Solar park size: 17,020 panels -> /", "Panels leased: 3,142 -> /", "Total kWh generated: 21,026,288 -> /", "Solar Angel donated panels: 1,015 -> /", "Remaining panels available: 12,863 -> /", "Subscription credits through 2038 -> /", "Solar Angel Fund partnership with The Community Foundation of Middle Tennessee -> /", "Target customers: homeowners and renters -> /", "Origin: customer demand for renewable options -> /", "Landfill reuse narrative -> /", "Public utility ownership stated -> /"], "company_type": "Installer / service company", "makes_products": "no", "type_evidence": "as a public utility, we want to make sure we are offering choices and opportunities that align with the city''s growth and needs", "founded_year": "", "employee_count": "", "cage_code": "", "min_order": "", "export_complianc